//! Test: defensive verbatim passthrough on parse-error input
//! Tags: annotations, strings, passthrough, recovery
//!
//! A bare string item after `@test` (`@test\n"desc"\nfn ...`) is NOT valid Rock:
//! a string literal cannot start an item. It is undocumented, used in zero real
//! files, and rejected by both skull-parser recovery and bone. `parse_module`
//! reports a recovery error, so `format_code` returns the input unchanged -- a
//! formatter must never emit corrupt or partial output for a file it cannot
//! fully parse. These cases pin that contract: unmodeled/invalid input passes
//! through byte-for-byte (so even `return;` is preserved, not normalized).

//=== TEST CASE: parse_error_input_passes_through_verbatim ===

@test
"some string"
fn test() {
    return;
}

//=== NO CHANGE ===

//=== TEST CASE: parse_error_preserves_valid_looking_body ===

@test
"test description for validation"
fn validate_input() {
    assert_eq(1 + 1, 2)
}

//=== NO CHANGE ===

//=== TEST CASE: parse_error_multiple_items_passthrough ===

@test
"first test case"
fn first_test() {
    return true
}

@test
"second test case with longer description"
fn second_test() {
    return false
}

//=== NO CHANGE ===
